1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,218
NeuroShield, by Team Paragon:

2
00:00:02,218 --> 00:00:05,755
a camera-free health monitor for astronauts, built for the

3
00:00:05,755 --> 00:00:07,341
NASA Space Apps Challenge.

4
00:00:07,891 --> 00:00:11,415
On the way to Mars, a message to Earth can take 22 minutes.

5
00:00:11,415 --> 00:00:14,700
When an astronaut’s health starts to slip, the crew has

6
00:00:14,700 --> 00:00:16,851
to notice first, and act on its own.

7
00:00:17,401 --> 00:00:19,254
Today’s monitors fall short.

8
00:00:19,254 --> 00:00:23,423
Cabin cameras can’t see behind helmets and masks, single-sensor

9
00:00:23,423 --> 00:00:27,327
headbands only detect fatigue, and periodic exams miss fast

10
00:00:27,327 --> 00:00:27,790
events.

11
00:00:28,340 --> 00:00:31,048
NeuroShield fuses four kinds of input:

12
00:00:31,048 --> 00:00:35,181
a wearable with EEG, brain-oxygen, pulse and skin sensors;

13
00:00:35,181 --> 00:00:37,176
standard vital-sign devices;

14
00:00:37,176 --> 00:00:39,884
cabin sensors; and short self-reports.

15
00:00:39,884 --> 00:00:42,164
It runs offline, in any browser.

16
00:00:42,714 --> 00:00:46,505
Together they give 20 live indicators across six body systems,

17
00:00:46,505 --> 00:00:49,562
including every vital sign the challenge asks for.

18
00:00:50,112 --> 00:00:51,761
Here is the live monitor.

19
00:00:51,761 --> 00:00:55,126
Each card is one signal, streaming once per second.

20
00:00:55,126 --> 00:00:58,820
The menu replays ten recorded mission scenarios at up to

21
00:00:58,820 --> 00:01:01,591
60× real speed, or connects a real device.

22
00:01:01,591 --> 00:01:05,418
The coloured strip above the timeline marks where problems

23
00:01:05,418 --> 00:01:05,813
occur.

24
00:01:06,363 --> 00:01:09,838
Let’s read one card. The large number is the current value,

25
00:01:09,838 --> 00:01:12,429
and the badge says normal, caution or alert.

26
00:01:12,429 --> 00:01:15,962
The green band and green dashed lines show the normal range;

27
00:01:15,962 --> 00:01:18,554
the red dotted lines are the caution limits.

28
00:01:18,554 --> 00:01:21,910
The faint dotted line is the session average, and the row

29
00:01:21,910 --> 00:01:25,267
below lists the average, minimum and maximum, next to the

30
00:01:25,267 --> 00:01:26,033
normal range.

31
00:01:26,583 --> 00:01:30,280
The fatigue scenario is a night shift at the robotic arm.

32
00:01:30,280 --> 00:01:34,041
The EEG drowsiness index rises long before the head starts

33
00:01:34,041 --> 00:01:37,998
to nod. NeuroShield raises a caution after about six minutes,

34
00:01:37,998 --> 00:01:41,111
almost five minutes before the first microsleep.

35
00:01:41,661 --> 00:01:45,340
In the cabin-leak scenario on Orion, pressure falls first,

36
00:01:45,340 --> 00:01:48,385
and NeuroShield warns before blood oxygen drops.

37
00:01:48,385 --> 00:01:52,001
When oxygen saturation falls below 90%, the hypoxia alert

38
00:01:52,001 --> 00:01:55,743
turns critical. Every alert shows the values that triggered

39
00:01:55,743 --> 00:01:59,296
it, numbered actions, its scientific source, and when to

40
00:01:59,296 --> 00:02:00,818
call the flight surgeon.

41
00:02:01,368 --> 00:02:05,188
Other scenarios cover a high-G entry, where brain oxygen

42
00:02:05,188 --> 00:02:08,598
falls with G-load; disorientation on a Mars rover;

43
00:02:08,598 --> 00:02:10,576
CO₂ build-up on a lunar base;

44
00:02:10,576 --> 00:02:12,691
low blood sugar after exercise;

45
00:02:12,691 --> 00:02:16,920
a fever; and a solar storm that sends the crew to the shelter.

46
00:02:17,470 --> 00:02:20,411
The health score summarises all 20 signals.

47
00:02:20,411 --> 00:02:24,242
Each body system blends its average and its worst value,

48
00:02:24,242 --> 00:02:27,525
so one dangerous reading is never averaged away.

49
00:02:27,525 --> 00:02:29,441
Active alerts cap the score:

50
00:02:29,441 --> 00:02:32,382
here, hypoxia pulls it down to 45, act now.

51
00:02:32,932 --> 00:02:36,357
Click any part of the body figure to see what it measures.

52
00:02:36,357 --> 00:02:38,069
The head shows brain signals;

53
00:02:38,069 --> 00:02:40,194
the heart, pulse and blood pressure;

54
00:02:40,194 --> 00:02:42,084
the lungs, oxygen and breathing;

55
00:02:42,084 --> 00:02:43,914
the arms and legs, temperature;

56
00:02:43,914 --> 00:02:46,571
and the habitat tag, cabin air and radiation.

57
00:02:46,571 --> 00:02:50,173
Each view lists live values and the spaceflight hazard behind

58
00:02:50,173 --> 00:02:50,468
them.

59
00:02:51,018 --> 00:02:54,755
Every morning, the daily check-in asks for vitals, glucose,

60
00:02:54,755 --> 00:02:56,782
mood, sleep, exercise and water.

61
00:02:56,782 --> 00:03:00,709
Tasks earn points, streaks and badges, and a 10-trial reaction

62
00:03:00,709 --> 00:03:04,257
test measures alertness, like the test astronauts use on

63
00:03:04,257 --> 00:03:05,397
the space station.

64
00:03:05,947 --> 00:03:08,590
Each entry goes into a personal logbook.

65
00:03:08,590 --> 00:03:12,424
NeuroShield compares every value with that astronaut’s own

66
00:03:12,424 --> 00:03:14,605
baseline, using a robust z-score.

67
00:03:14,605 --> 00:03:18,438
Crew D’s body mass slowly drifts down, and from day 34 the

68
00:03:18,438 --> 00:03:20,091
detector flags it in red.

69
00:03:20,641 --> 00:03:24,328
The anomaly map shows 17 indicators over 60 days:

70
00:03:24,328 --> 00:03:27,564
red means above baseline, blue means below.

71
00:03:27,564 --> 00:03:31,853
You can spot the CO₂ event on days 30–33, the solar storm

72
00:03:31,853 --> 00:03:35,766
on day 47, and crew D’s late fall in sleep and mood.

73
00:03:36,316 --> 00:03:40,038
Flagged days become episodes, with a likely cause and advice.

74
00:03:40,038 --> 00:03:43,638
For crew C, raised temperature and heart rate, with falling

75
00:03:43,638 --> 00:03:46,445
heart-rate variability, point to an infection.

76
00:03:46,445 --> 00:03:49,923
A detector check compares every result with the dataset’s

77
00:03:49,923 --> 00:03:50,716
ground truth.

78
00:03:51,266 --> 00:03:53,835
Finally, the guide explains every indicator:

79
00:03:53,835 --> 00:03:57,162
how it is measured, its normal and caution limits, why it

80
00:03:57,162 --> 00:03:59,906
matters in space, and the literature behind it.

81
00:04:00,456 --> 00:04:04,431
All of this runs on an open, labelled dataset we created:

82
00:04:04,431 --> 00:04:08,337
ten 15-minute scenarios at 1 Hz, and 60 days of logs for

83
00:04:08,337 --> 00:04:11,336
four crew members, with 13 injected events.

84
00:04:11,886 --> 00:04:16,266
On this synthetic data, the alerts reach 92% sensitivity

85
00:04:16,266 --> 00:04:20,803
and 89% specificity, with no false alarms on a normal day.

86
00:04:21,353 --> 00:04:25,738
We documented the work in a 7-page, IEEE-style paper with

87
00:04:25,738 --> 00:04:26,815
44 references.

88
00:04:27,365 --> 00:04:29,483
It first reviews the literature:

89
00:04:29,483 --> 00:04:33,058
how spaceflight changes the heart, immunity and sleep;

90
00:04:33,058 --> 00:04:35,705
why CO₂ causes headaches on the station;

91
00:04:35,705 --> 00:04:39,346
and how EEG and near-infrared sensors track fatigue and

92
00:04:39,346 --> 00:04:40,869
brain oxygen in pilots.

93
00:04:41,419 --> 00:04:44,676
It then defines the health score, the fourteen rules with

94
00:04:44,676 --> 00:04:47,819
persistence, hysteresis and root-cause suppression, and

95
00:04:47,819 --> 00:04:49,419
the robust anomaly detector.

96
00:04:49,969 --> 00:04:54,023
The results show drowsiness flagged 4.5 minutes before the

97
00:04:54,023 --> 00:04:58,148
first microsleep, and cabin-pressure loss 96 seconds before

98
00:04:58,148 --> 00:05:02,482
blood oxygen falls. The health score averages 96 when nominal,

99
00:05:02,482 --> 00:05:03,601
and 58 when not.

100
00:05:04,151 --> 00:05:07,407
The logbook detector finds all 13 injected events.

101
00:05:07,407 --> 00:05:10,077
The paper is also clear about its limits:

102
00:05:10,077 --> 00:05:14,049
the data are synthetic, thresholds need personal calibration,

103
00:05:14,049 --> 00:05:16,524
and activity context is still missing.

104
00:05:17,074 --> 00:05:20,687
Next, we connect the real NeuroShield wearable, and validate

105
00:05:20,687 --> 00:05:22,192
it on a motion simulator.

106
00:05:22,192 --> 00:05:24,541
NeuroShield: see it before you feel it.
